{sentence}
What is Color Gamut?
{sentence}
Color gamut is defined as the range of colors which a particular device can produce or record.
{sentence}
It is usually shown by an enclosed area of the primary colors of the device on the chromaticity diagram.
{sentence}
For example, the primary colors of monitors are red, green, and blue. Hence, the color gamut of a monitor is shown in a triangular area enclosed by the color coordinates of the monitor's red, green, and blue colors.
{sentence}
A chromaticity diagram with different color gamuts is shown in Figure 1.

{Sentence 1: What is Color Gamut?}
{Sentence 2: Color gamut is defined as the range of colors which a particular device can produce or record.}
{Sentence 3: Color gamut is usually shown by an enclosed area of the primary colors of the device on the chromaticity diagram.}
{Sentence 4: Commonly Seen Color Gamut Standards include NTSC, sRGB, AdobeRGB, DCI-P3, EBU, and AppleRGB.}
{Sentence 5: The selection of color gamut really depends on your workflow and requirements.}

